    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **James Wood** (76.28) and **Brice Turang** (72.70) anchoring the lineup, standing as our two most efficient options. **James Wood** benefits from a rare combination of elite xwOBA metrics (.426) and a favorable matchup against Eduardo Rodriguez, who presents a high 4.84 xERA, allowing for significant upside in power and run production. Meanwhile, **Brice Turang** is positioned for a breakout performance against Tomoyuki Sugano; the heavy SP skill edge (+19%) makes him a must-start, as he remains vital to maintaining our lead in the scarce stolen base category. **Tyler Soderstrom** (69.68) also provides immense value hitting second, as the matchup against Zebby Matthews is mitigated by positive platoon splits and favorable batting order placement.\n\nThe most challenging decision today involved keeping **Mickey Moniak** (45.86) on the bench. While his projection remained respectable, he fell just short of our daily threshold due to a daunting -15% penalty from the dynamic platoon disadvantage against Shane Drohan. The optimizer favored the upside of **Jose Caballero** (53.79), whose stolen base potential remains the primary tie-breaker given our team strategy. We are also closely monitoring the statuses of **Gabriel Moreno**, **Isaac Paredes**, **Max Muncy**, **Cole Young**, **Austin Riley**, **Corbin Carroll**, **Seiya Suzuki**, and **Josh Naylor**, as all currently operate under \"Lineup Pending\" designations. **Corbin Carroll** remains a core play despite a tougher matchup against Carson Palmquist, as his elite sprint speed and favorable slotting at leadoff ensure he stays in the lineup despite the -13% SP skill headwind. Investors should note that **Vinnie Pasquantino** was excluded primarily due to the severe matchup against Tarik Skubal (2.66 xERA), which rendered his -19% SP skill penalty too steep to justify over our primary starters.",
